WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.430
Every network tool, every attack, every alert you'll ever see, lives somewhere on this map.

2
00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:11.130
It's called the OSI model, and it breaks a network into seven layers.

3
00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:16.410
Learn it once, and suddenly every tool you touch finally has a place to sit.

4
00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:20.200
The whole idea is separation of concerns.

5
00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:25.620
Each layer does one job, and only talks to the layer directly above and below it.

6
00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:33.510
At the top, Layer 7, Application: the programs people actually use, like a browser, DNS, or email.

7
00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:40.130
Layer 6, Presentation: formatting and encryption — this is where TLS lives.

8
00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:46.900
Layer 5, Session: it opens, manages, and closes the conversation between two machines.

9
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:53.640
Layer 4, Transport: TCP and UDP, port numbers, and reliable delivery.

10
00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:59.090
Layer 3, Network: IP addresses and routing packets between networks.

11
00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:05.260
Layer 2, Data Link: MAC addresses, switches, and frames on the local network.

12
00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:12.360
And at the bottom, Layer 1, Physical: the cables, the radio, the electrical signals — the raw bits.

13
00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:15.600
Here's why this matters for a SOC analyst.

14
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:21.050
When an alert fires, the layer tells you what kind of attack it is, and which tool would even see it.

15
00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:28.560
A SQL injection or a phishing page is a Layer 7 attack — that's what a web application firewall watches.

16
00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:34.660
A port scan or a SYN flood is Layer 4 — the firewall and IDS live here.

17
00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:38.960
IP spoofing is Layer 3, routing between networks.

18
00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:43.860
ARP spoofing and MAC flooding are Layer 2, down on the local switch.

19
00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:48.710
And a physical tap on the wire is the lowest layer of all, Layer 1.

20
00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:52.910
So the OSI model isn't trivia to memorize.

21
00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:59.410
It's the shared language the whole industry uses to say exactly where something is happening.

22
00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:03.180
Know the layer, and you already know half the investigation.
